---
import LearnArticle from "../../components/LearnArticle.astro";
import DemoFrame from "../../components/DemoFrame.astro";

const growth = [
	{ items: "10", comparisons: "45" },
	{ items: "100", comparisons: "4,950" },
	{ items: "1,000", comparisons: "499,500" },
	{ items: "1 million", comparisons: "about 500 billion" },
];
---

<LearnArticle
	href="/learn/sorting-algorithms"
	description="How sorting algorithms work: step through a bubble sort one comparison at a time, count the swaps, and see why some sorting methods are much faster than others."
	lead="Computers sort things constantly — search results, playlists, prices from low to high. Here's how one of the simplest methods works, one step at a time."
>
	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">Why Sorting Matters</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			A sorted list is easier for people to read — and far faster for computers to search. The{" "}
			<a href="/learn/binary-search">Binary Search</a> trick of "guess the middle, throw away half" only
			works when the list is already in order. Sorting once makes every search after that quick.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			A step-by-step method for solving a problem is called an <em>algorithm</em>. There are many
			sorting algorithms. They all end up with the same sorted list — they just take different routes to
			get there, and some routes are much shorter than others.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">Bubble Sort: Compare Neighbors</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Picture a row of people you want to arrange from shortest to tallest. Bubble sort works like this:
		</p>
		<ol class="block-list">
			<li>Look at the first two people. If the left one is taller, swap them.</li>
			<li>Move one place to the right and compare the next pair. Swap if needed.</li>
			<li>
				Keep going to the end of the row. The tallest person has now "bubbled up" all the way to the
				end — they're in their final place.
			</li>
			<li>Start again from the left, ignoring the end of the row that's already sorted.</li>
			<li>Stop when you get through a whole pass without making a single swap.</li>
		</ol>
		<p class="block-callout">
			Each pass moves the biggest remaining item to its correct place — like a bubble rising to the top.
		</p>
	</section>

	<DemoFrame label="Bubble sort demo">
		<div class="bsort-demo" data-bsort-demo>
			<div class="bsort-legend" aria-hidden="true">
				<span><span class="bsort-key bsort-key--compare"></span>Being compared</span>
				<span><span class="bsort-key bsort-key--done"></span>In its final place</span>
			</div>

			<div class="bsort-stage" data-bsort-stage role="img" aria-label="Bars to be sorted"></div>

			<div class="demo-stats">
				<div class="demo-stat">
					<span class="demo-stat-value" data-bsort-comparisons>0</span>
					<span class="demo-stat-label">Comparisons</span>
				</div>
				<div class="demo-stat">
					<span class="demo-stat-value" data-bsort-swaps>0</span>
					<span class="demo-stat-label">Swaps</span>
				</div>
				<div class="demo-stat">
					<span class="demo-stat-value" data-bsort-passes>0</span>
					<span class="demo-stat-label">Passes</span>
				</div>
			</div>

			<label class="bsort-predict-toggle">
				<input type="checkbox" data-bsort-predict checked />
				Let me predict each step
				<span class="bsort-predict-score" data-bsort-predict-score></span>
			</label>

			<div class="bsort-ask" data-bsort-ask hidden>
				<p class="bsort-question" data-bsort-question></p>
				<div class="demo-controls bsort-choices">
					<button type="button" class="demo-btn demo-btn--primary" data-bsort-choice="swap">Swap them</button>
					<button type="button" class="demo-btn demo-btn--primary" data-bsort-choice="keep">Leave them</button>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="demo-controls">
				<button type="button" class="demo-btn demo-btn--primary" data-bsort-step>Next step</button>
				<button type="button" class="demo-btn" data-bsort-auto>Auto-sort</button>
				<button type="button" class="demo-btn" data-bsort-shuffle>Shuffle</button>
			</div>

			<div class="demo-field">
				<label for="bsort-count">Bars</label>
				<select id="bsort-count" class="demo-select" data-bsort-count>
					<option value="6">6</option>
					<option value="10" selected>10</option>
					<option value="16">16</option>
				</select>
				<label for="bsort-speed">Speed</label>
				<input id="bsort-speed" class="demo-range bsort-speed" type="range" min="1" max="5" value="3" data-bsort-speed />
			</div>

			<p class="demo-status" data-bsort-status aria-live="polite"></p>
		</div>
	</DemoFrame>

	<section class="block block--example">
		<p class="block-title">How Much Work Is That?</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			In the worst case — a list that starts completely backwards — bubble sort has to compare nearly
			every item with nearly every other item. The work grows much faster than the list does:
		</p>
		<div class="table-wrap">
			<table class="data-table">
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th>Items to sort</th>
						<th>Comparisons (worst case)</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					{
						growth.map((row) => (
							<tr>
								<td class="num">{row.items}</td>
								<td class="num">{row.comparisons}</td>
							</tr>
						))
					}
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p class="block-text">
			Ten times more items means roughly <em>a hundred times</em> more work. For a class list, that's no
			problem. For a million online orders, it's far too slow.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">Faster Ways to Sort</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Because sorting matters so much, computer scientists have invented quicker methods. One of the
			best known is <em>merge sort</em>, which uses a "divide and conquer" idea:
		</p>
		<ul class="block-list">
			<li>Split the list in half, and keep splitting until every piece is just one item.</li>
			<li>
				Merge the pieces back together two at a time, always keeping them in order — like two people
				each holding a sorted hand of cards, combining them by repeatedly taking the smaller front card.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p class="block-text">
			Sorting a million items takes merge sort around 20 million steps instead of 500 billion. Same
			sorted result, a tiny fraction of the work.
		</p>
		<p class="block-callout">
			Different algorithms can solve the same problem correctly — but how much work they take can be
			wildly different. Choosing a good algorithm matters.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">Fast Enough — and Far Too Slow</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Bubble sort is slow on huge lists, but it still finishes in a sensible amount of time. Computer
			scientists call that <em>reasonable time</em>: as the list grows, the work grows by multiplying (10×
			more items, about 100× more work).
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Some problems are much worse. To try every possible order of a delivery driver's stops, adding just
			<em>one</em> more stop multiplies the work again. With 10 stops there are over 3 million orders; with
			20 there are more than a billion billion. That's <em>unreasonable time</em> — no computer could ever
			finish checking them all.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			For problems like that, programmers use a <em>heuristic</em>: a sensible shortcut that finds a good
			answer quickly, even if it isn't guaranteed to be the very best. For the driver, "always go to the
			nearest stop next" is a heuristic.
		</p>
	</section>
</LearnArticle>

<script>
	const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-bsort-demo]")!;
	const stage = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-bsort-stage]")!;
	const stepBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-bsort-step]")!;
	const autoBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-bsort-auto]")!;
	const shuffleBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-bsort-shuffle]")!;
	const countSelect = root.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>("[data-bsort-count]")!;
	const speedInput = root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("[data-bsort-speed]")!;
	const statusEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-bsort-status]")!;
	const comparisonsEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-bsort-comparisons]")!;
	const swapsEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-bsort-swaps]")!;
	const passesEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-bsort-passes]")!;
	const predictToggle = root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("[data-bsort-predict]")!;
	const predictScoreEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-bsort-predict-score]")!;
	const askEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-bsort-ask]")!;
	const questionEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-bsort-question]")!;
	const choiceBtns = root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-bsort-choice]");
	let predictRight = 0;
	let predictTotal = 0;
	let askTimer: number | undefined;

	interface Item {
		id: number;
		value: number;
		el: HTMLElement;
	}

	let items: Item[] = [];
	let pass = 0; // completed passes
	let j = 0; // left index of the next pair to compare
	let swappedThisPass = false;
	let comparisons = 0;
	let swaps = 0;
	let sortedFrom = 0; // items at index >= sortedFrom are in their final place
	let done = false;
	let comparing: [number, number] | null = null;
	let autoTimer: number | null = null;

	function shuffle() {
		stopAuto();
		const n = Number(countSelect.value);
		// Distinct values so every bar has a clearly different height.
		const pool = Array.from({ length: 30 }, (_, i) => i + 1);
		for (let i = pool.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
			const k = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
			[pool[i], pool[k]] = [pool[k], pool[i]];
		}
		const values = pool.slice(0, n);
		stage.innerHTML = "";
		items = values.map((value, id) => {
			const el = document.createElement("div");
			el.className = "bsort-bar";
			el.innerHTML = `<span class="bsort-bar-fill"></span><span class="bsort-bar-label">${value}</span>`;
			stage.append(el);
			return { id, value, el };
		});
		pass = 0;
		j = 0;
		swappedThisPass = false;
		comparisons = 0;
		swaps = 0;
		sortedFrom = n;
		done = false;
		comparing = null;
		render();
		predictRight = 0;
		predictTotal = 0;
		statusEl.textContent = predictToggle.checked
			? "Bubble sort compares neighbors, left to right. You decide each time."
			: "Press “Next step” to compare the first two bars.";
		prepare();
	}

	function render() {
		const n = items.length;
		const max = Math.max(...items.map((it) => it.value));
		stage.style.setProperty("--bars", String(n));
		items.forEach((item, index) => {
			item.el.style.setProperty("--i", String(index));
			item.el.style.setProperty("--h", String(item.value / max));
			item.el.classList.toggle("is-compared", !!comparing && (index === comparing[0] || index === comparing[1]));
			item.el.classList.toggle("is-done", index >= sortedFrom);
		});
		stage.setAttribute("aria-label", `Bars in current order: ${items.map((it) => it.value).join(", ")}`);
		comparisonsEl.textContent = String(comparisons);
		swapsEl.textContent = String(swaps);
		passesEl.textContent = String(pass);
		stepBtn.disabled = done;
		autoBtn.disabled = done;
		autoBtn.textContent = autoTimer === null ? "Auto-sort" : "Pause";
	}

	function finish() {
		done = true;
		sortedFrom = 0;
		comparing = null;
		stopAuto();
		render();
		askEl.hidden = true;
		stepBtn.hidden = false;
		const predicted = predictTotal ? ` You predicted ${predictRight} of ${predictTotal} steps correctly.` : "";
		statusEl.textContent = `Sorted! ${comparisons} comparisons and ${swaps} swaps.${predicted} Press “Shuffle” to try a new list.`;
	}

	function step() {
		if (done) return;
		const n = items.length;
		const lastIndex = n - 1 - pass; // the pass ends once j reaches here

		if (j >= lastIndex) {
			// End of a pass: the biggest remaining bar is now in place.
			pass += 1;
			sortedFrom = lastIndex;
			if (!swappedThisPass || pass >= n - 1) {
				finish();
				if (!swappedThisPass && pass < n - 1) {
					statusEl.textContent = `A whole pass with no swaps — so everything is already in order. Sorted with ${comparisons} comparisons and ${swaps} swaps.`;
				}
				return;
			}
			j = 0;
			swappedThisPass = false;
			comparing = null;
			render();
			statusEl.textContent = `Pass ${pass} done — ${items[lastIndex].value} has bubbled to its final place. Starting the next pass.`;
			return;
		}

		const a = items[j];
		const b = items[j + 1];
		comparisons += 1;
		comparing = [j, j + 1];
		if (a.value > b.value) {
			items[j] = b;
			items[j + 1] = a;
			swaps += 1;
			swappedThisPass = true;
			statusEl.textContent = `${a.value} is bigger than ${b.value}, so they swap.`;
		} else {
			statusEl.textContent = `${a.value} is smaller than ${b.value} — already in order, no swap.`;
		}
		j += 1;
		render();
	}

	// --- Predict mode: before each comparison, the student decides whether
	// the pair should swap, then sees the step happen. Passes the pairs to
	// the student only while stepping by hand (Auto-sort just runs).
	function nextIsComparison() {
		return !done && j < items.length - 1 - pass;
	}

	function prepare() {
		window.clearTimeout(askTimer);
		predictScoreEl.textContent = predictTotal ? `— ${predictRight} of ${predictTotal} right` : "";
		const ask = predictToggle.checked && autoTimer === null && nextIsComparison();
		askEl.hidden = !ask;
		stepBtn.hidden = ask;
		choiceBtns.forEach((b) => (b.disabled = false));
		if (ask) {
			comparing = [j, j + 1];
			render();
			questionEl.textContent = `Should ${items[j].value} and ${items[j + 1].value} swap? (Smaller numbers belong on the left.)`;
		}
	}

	choiceBtns.forEach((btn) => {
		btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
			if (!nextIsComparison()) return;
			choiceBtns.forEach((b) => (b.disabled = true));
			const shouldSwap = items[j].value > items[j + 1].value;
			const right = (btn.dataset.bsortChoice === "swap") === shouldSwap;
			predictTotal += 1;
			if (right) predictRight += 1;
			step();
			statusEl.textContent = `${right ? "✓ Right!" : "✗ Not this time."} ${statusEl.textContent}`;
			// Pause so the swap (or non-swap) can be seen before the next question.
			askTimer = window.setTimeout(prepare, 750);
		});
	});

	predictToggle.addEventListener("change", () => {
		if (!predictToggle.checked) comparing = null;
		render();
		prepare();
	});

	function stopAuto() {
		if (autoTimer !== null) {
			window.clearInterval(autoTimer);
			autoTimer = null;
		}
	}

	function speedMs() {
		return [900, 600, 380, 200, 90][Number(speedInput.value) - 1];
	}

	function startAuto() {
		stopAuto();
		window.clearTimeout(askTimer);
		askEl.hidden = true;
		stepBtn.hidden = false;
		autoTimer = window.setInterval(step, speedMs());
		render();
	}

	stepBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
		stopAuto();
		step();
		prepare();
	});
	autoBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
		if (autoTimer === null) startAuto();
		else {
			stopAuto();
			render();
			prepare();
		}
	});
	speedInput.addEventListener("input", () => {
		if (autoTimer !== null) startAuto();
	});
	shuffleBtn.addEventListener("click", shuffle);
	countSelect.addEventListener("change", shuffle);

	shuffle();
</script>

<style is:global>
	.bsort-demo .bsort-predict-toggle {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 0.5em;
		margin-top: 0.9em;
		min-height: 2.2em;
		font-size: 0.95em;
		font-weight: 600;
		color: var(--color-text);
		cursor: pointer;
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-predict-toggle input {
		width: 1.2em;
		height: 1.2em;
		accent-color: var(--color-accent);
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-predict-score {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		color: var(--color-accent);
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-question {
		margin: 0.8em 0 0;
		text-align: center;
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 1.05em;
		color: #fff3d6;
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-choices {
		margin-top: 0.5em;
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-legend {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		justify-content: center;
		gap: 0.4em 1.2em;
		margin-bottom: 0.8em;
		font-size: 0.85em;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-legend > span {
		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.4em;
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-key {
		display: inline-block;
		width: 1.1em;
		height: 1.1em;
		border-radius: 3px;
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-key--compare {
		background: #5b6075;
		outline: 3px solid #fff3d6;
		outline-offset: 1px;
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-key--done {
		background: var(--color-accent);
	}

	/* Bars are absolutely positioned by index (--i) so a swap slides them
	   past each other instead of just redrawing. */
	.bsort-demo .bsort-stage {
		--bars: 10;
		position: relative;
		height: 14em;
		margin: 0 auto;
		max-width: 44em;
		padding-bottom: 1.8em;
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-bar {
		position: absolute;
		bottom: 0;
		left: calc(var(--i) * (100% / var(--bars)));
		width: calc(100% / var(--bars));
		height: 100%;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		justify-content: flex-end;
		align-items: center;
		padding: 0 0.15em;
		transition: left 0.3s ease;
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-bar-fill {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		max-width: 3.2em;
		height: calc(var(--h) * (100% - 1.8em));
		min-height: 0.4em;
		border-radius: 5px 5px 2px 2px;
		background: #5b6075;
		transition:
			height 0.3s ease,
			background-color 0.2s ease;
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-bar-label {
		height: 1.8em;
		line-height: 1.8em;
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 0.9em;
		color: var(--color-text);
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-bar.is-done .bsort-bar-fill {
		background: var(--color-accent);
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-bar.is-compared .bsort-bar-fill {
		outline: 3px solid #fff3d6;
		outline-offset: 1px;
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-bar.is-compared .bsort-bar-label {
		color: #fff3d6;
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
		text-underline-offset: 3px;
	}

	.bsort-demo .bsort-speed {
		width: 9em;
	}

	.demo-frame.is-fullscreen .bsort-demo .bsort-stage {
		height: 16em;
	}
</style>
